WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.040 --> 00:00:06.310
What do imaginary dogs, monsters from another
dimension, and a live decapitation have in

00:00:06.310 --> 00:00:07.310
common?

00:00:07.310 --> 00:00:10.590
They're all from projects starring these former
Lord of the Rings stars.

00:00:10.590 --> 00:00:12.740
Here's what the Lord Of The Rings looks like
now.

00:00:12.740 --> 00:00:13.740
"End?

00:00:13.740 --> 00:00:17.950
No, the journey doesn't end here."

00:00:17.950 --> 00:00:22.090
Since "The Lord of the Rings" days, Elijah
Wood has been an instantly-recognizable star

00:00:22.090 --> 00:00:26.610
and has had a delightfully eclectic résumé,
doing everything from high-profile films to

00:00:26.610 --> 00:00:29.960
animated voiceover roles to quirky television
series.

00:00:29.960 --> 00:00:34.820
Wood provided the voices for Mumble in Dreamworks'
family-friendly "Happy Feet" and Wirt in Cartoon

00:00:34.820 --> 00:00:37.120
Network's spooky "Over the Garden Wall."

00:00:37.120 --> 00:00:42.040
He starred in the series "Dirk Gently's Holistic
Detective Agency" and "Wilfred," and has appeared

00:00:42.040 --> 00:00:47.230
in films such as "Eternal Sunshine of the
Spotless Mind," "Sin City," and "I Don't Feel

00:00:47.230 --> 00:00:48.670
at Home in This World Anymore."

00:00:48.670 --> 00:00:53.590
“Makes me so furious!

00:00:53.590 --> 00:00:55.420
Kevin stop it!”

00:00:55.420 --> 00:00:59.400
Most recently, Wood has lent his voice acting
talents to the Disney Channel animated series

00:00:59.400 --> 00:01:03.990
"Star Wars Resistance" and Cartoon Network's
"Summer Camp Island," but has also stayed

00:01:03.990 --> 00:01:11.010
busy in live action roles, starring in "Come
to Daddy" in 2019 and "No Man of God" in 2021.

00:01:11.010 --> 00:01:15.490
Perhaps best known for his role as Frodo’s
right-hand man, Samwise Gamgee, actor Sean

00:01:15.490 --> 00:01:20.220
Astin has changed a lot since portraying the
loyal and tender-hearted hobbit.

00:01:20.220 --> 00:01:23.960
Prior to "The Lord of the Rings," Astin was
known for playing Mikey Walsh in the classic

00:01:23.960 --> 00:01:28.420
children's adventure film "The Goonies," and
as the title character in famous Notre Dame

00:01:28.420 --> 00:01:30.490
football walk-on story "Rudy."

00:01:30.490 --> 00:01:33.869
Since "The Lord of The Rings," though, Astin
hasn't returned to the film spotlight, appearing

00:01:33.869 --> 00:01:38.460
only in smaller supporting roles in movies
like the Adam Sandler comedies "50 First Dates"

00:01:38.460 --> 00:01:39.460
and "Click."

00:01:39.460 --> 00:01:43.510
Instead, Astin has done a lot of voiceover
work for various animated series, lending

00:01:43.510 --> 00:01:48.310
his vocal talents to everything from "Teenage
Mutant Ninja Turtles" to "Captain Underpants."

00:01:48.310 --> 00:01:52.869
Astin has also guest-starred on a wide variety
of television series, including "Law &amp; Order,"

00:01:52.869 --> 00:01:56.780
"24," and "Brooklyn Nine-Nine" — and he's
been part of the cast of various live-action

00:01:56.780 --> 00:01:57.780
miniseries.

00:01:57.780 --> 00:02:03.119
He played Jim Kent on the FX series "The Strain,"
Sunny Gryce on HBO's "Perry Mason," and most

00:02:03.119 --> 00:02:07.500
notably Bob Newby in Netflix's mega-hit series
"Stranger Things."

00:02:07.500 --> 00:02:12.489
Astin also starred in the Netflix original
sitcom "No Good Nick," which ran for two seasons.

00:02:12.489 --> 00:02:17.260
Undoubtedly the most decorated of all the
Lord of the Rings cast members, Sir Ian McKellen

00:02:17.260 --> 00:02:22.010
only looks all the more wiser than when he
played legendary wizard Gandalf the White

00:02:22.010 --> 00:02:23.900
— and, previously, Gandalf the Grey.

00:02:23.900 --> 00:02:28.049
The knighted actor has more awards than you
can shake a wizard's staff at.

00:02:28.049 --> 00:02:32.349
McKellen won a Golden Globe Award for his
role in the Made for TV movie Rasputin.

00:02:32.349 --> 00:02:37.681
He’s also won two Saturn Awards, an Annie
award, an Honorary Golden Berlin Bear, two

00:02:37.681 --> 00:02:42.879
British Independent Film Awards, two Critics
Choice Awards, two Screen Actors Guild Awards…

00:02:42.879 --> 00:02:44.640
and the list only goes on from there.

00:02:44.640 --> 00:02:49.400
He is an accomplished stage actor, in addition
to one of the big screen's biggest stars — with

00:02:49.400 --> 00:02:53.709
The Lord of the Rings merely being another
accomplishment to add to his already extensive

00:02:53.709 --> 00:02:55.450
résumé of fine films.

00:02:55.450 --> 00:02:58.370
“Here, the hammer stroke will fall hardest.”

00:02:58.370 --> 00:03:02.870
McKellen reprised his role as Gandalf in "The
Hobbit" trilogy of films, and has also had

00:03:02.870 --> 00:03:06.749
an impressive run as Magneto in Fox's "X-Men"
films.

00:03:06.749 --> 00:03:11.609
More recently, he has starred in the National
Theater Live production of King Lear in 2018,

00:03:11.609 --> 00:03:16.720
as Gus the Theatre Cat in the movie musical
"Cats," and as a con artist opposite Dame

00:03:16.720 --> 00:03:20.569
Helen Mirren in the 2019 film "The Good Liar."

00:03:20.569 --> 00:03:24.419
Although Boromir will always be known as the
one member of the Fellowship who tried to

00:03:24.419 --> 00:03:29.010
take the ring from Frodo, Sean Bean imbued
the character with warmth and nobility.

00:03:29.010 --> 00:03:31.870
“What have I done?

00:03:31.870 --> 00:03:33.309
Please, Frodo.”

00:03:33.309 --> 00:03:37.609
His loss at the end of "The Fellowship of
the Ring" still feels tragic despite his earlier

00:03:37.609 --> 00:03:38.609
actions.

00:03:38.609 --> 00:03:41.839
Following "The Lord of the Rings," Bean went
on to appear in numerous films, including

00:03:41.839 --> 00:03:47.189
"Troy," "National Treasure," and "Percy Jackson
&amp; the Olympians: The Lightning Thief."

00:03:47.189 --> 00:03:51.430
In 2011, the actor took on what would become
his most notable role as leading man Ned Stark

00:03:51.430 --> 00:03:55.439
on the first season of "Game of Thrones,"
the hit TV-adaptation of George R.R.

00:03:55.439 --> 00:03:58.680
Martin's popular "A Song of Ice and Fire"
book series.

00:03:58.680 --> 00:04:04.180
However, spoiler alert, he gets his head lopped
off in the penultimate episode of the debut

00:04:04.180 --> 00:04:05.180
season.

00:04:05.180 --> 00:04:08.769
Although playing characters who meet tragic
ends is nothing new for Bean, as the actor

00:04:08.769 --> 00:04:13.559
is notorious for being killed off in what
seems like every role he's ever played.

00:04:13.559 --> 00:04:17.570
Since "Game of Thrones," Bean has continued
to appear in significant roles in films such

00:04:17.570 --> 00:04:21.430
as "Jupiter Ascending" and "The Martian,"
although his work has taken a turn toward

00:04:21.430 --> 00:04:23.880
television in more recent years.

00:04:23.880 --> 00:04:28.700
After starring in the BBC series "Broken"
in 2017, Bean went on to appear in series

00:04:28.700 --> 00:04:32.550
such as "The Frankenstein Chronicles," "World
on Fire," and "Snowpiercer."

00:04:32.550 --> 00:04:37.770
A household name today, Orlando Bloom is no
longer the immortal, baby-faced elven bowman

00:04:37.770 --> 00:04:41.650
we first met in "The Lord of the Rings" trilogy
— despite efforts to make him appear that

00:04:41.650 --> 00:04:43.150
way in "The Hobbit" films.

00:04:43.150 --> 00:04:47.010
For a while following "The Lord of the Rings,"
Bloom continued to gravitate toward grand

00:04:47.010 --> 00:04:48.960
scale, combat-heavy blockbusters.

00:04:48.960 --> 00:04:52.520
He starred in the first three films in the
"Pirates of the Caribbean" series, co-starred

00:04:52.520 --> 00:04:58.250
alongside Brad Pitt and Eric Bana in "Troy,"
and led the historical epic "Kingdom of Heaven."

00:04:58.250 --> 00:05:02.230
In recent years, though, Bloom has drifted
toward more tonally-diverse projects, starring

00:05:02.230 --> 00:05:07.540
in the disturbing trauma drama "Retaliation"
in 2017 and the gritty war film "The Outpost"

00:05:07.540 --> 00:05:08.540
in 2020.

00:05:08.540 --> 00:05:12.930
Bloom has also made the jump to television,
starring in Amazon's Victorian fantasy series

00:05:12.930 --> 00:05:17.480
"Carnival Row" and voicing Prince Harry in
HBO's animated series "The Prince."

00:05:17.480 --> 00:05:22.790
And he is in the reality-bending 2021 time
travel drama "Needle in a Timestack," in which

00:05:22.790 --> 00:05:27.810
he plays a romantic rival to Leslie Odom Jr.'s
temporally-challenged protagonist.

00:05:27.810 --> 00:05:31.690
He’s known for playing the more serious
of the two Hobbits who decided to tag along

00:05:31.690 --> 00:05:34.580
with the Fellowship — despite not being
invited.

00:05:34.580 --> 00:05:39.920
Dominic Monaghan, the actor behind Meriadoc
Brandybuck isn't quite the pipe-weed smoking

00:05:39.920 --> 00:05:43.280
halfling we remember from "The Lord of the
Rings" films.

00:05:43.280 --> 00:05:46.370
Following "The Lord of the Rings," Monaghan
went on to play Charlie Pace in the first

00:05:46.370 --> 00:05:51.260
three seasons of ABC's juggernaut series "Lost,"
a character that possibly rivaled even Merry

00:05:51.260 --> 00:05:52.740
in notability.

00:05:52.740 --> 00:05:56.880
The English actor also played Chris Bradley
in "X-Men Origins: Wolverine," along with

00:05:56.880 --> 00:06:00.770
a number of other film roles that are much
less well known.

00:06:00.770 --> 00:06:04.900
After his time on "Lost," Monaghan had regular
roles on numerous television series, including

00:06:04.900 --> 00:06:08.230
"Flashforward," "100 Code," and "Quantum Break."

00:06:08.230 --> 00:06:12.390
Monaghan also spent several years hosting
"Wild Things with Dominic Monaghan," a wildlife

00:06:12.390 --> 00:06:15.760
documentary series nominated for numerous
awards.

00:06:15.760 --> 00:06:20.420
In recent years, Monaghan has continued to
act, appearing in a small role in "Star Wars:

00:06:20.420 --> 00:06:23.600
Episode IX – The Rise of Skywalker," along
with several smaller projects.

00:06:23.600 --> 00:06:28.110
He also has appeared on numerous podcasts,
and still hosts one, along with "The Lord

00:06:28.110 --> 00:06:32.210
of the Rings" co-star Billy Boyd called "The
Friendship Onion."

00:06:32.210 --> 00:06:36.620
You'd be forgiven for not being able to name
another film featuring Billy Boyd, the actor

00:06:36.620 --> 00:06:39.740
behind "The Lord of the Rings'" Peregrin "Pippin"
Took.

00:06:39.740 --> 00:06:43.950
No doubt, it would be due to not recognizing
the middle-aged actor because he always manages

00:06:43.950 --> 00:06:47.230
to look younger than he really is, much like
a Hobbit.

00:06:47.230 --> 00:06:51.170
Boyd's biggest role, by far, was playing the
most confused, pipe-weed toking Hobbit in

00:06:51.170 --> 00:06:52.460
the Fellowship.

00:06:52.460 --> 00:06:55.740
Although Boyd continued to act following "The
Lord of the Rings," his roles have not been

00:06:55.740 --> 00:06:57.360
nearly as memorable.

00:06:57.360 --> 00:07:01.840
His most notable part was playing Barrett
Bonden in the Russell Crowe sea epic "Master

00:07:01.840 --> 00:07:04.150
and Commander: The Far Side of the World."

00:07:04.150 --> 00:07:07.610
But don't be too sad for Boyd, since his true
passion is music.

00:07:07.610 --> 00:07:11.570
The famous Hobbit is the front-man for a band
called Beecake, playing guitar and providing

00:07:11.570 --> 00:07:12.700
vocals.

00:07:12.700 --> 00:07:16.310
Boyd also wrote and performed the end-credits
theme for "The Hobbit: The Battle of the Five

00:07:16.310 --> 00:07:20.300
Armies," further lending his influence to
Middle-earth and providing trivia material

00:07:20.300 --> 00:07:22.330
for die-hard Hobbit fans.

00:07:22.330 --> 00:07:26.320
In recent years, Boyd has guest-starred on
a number of television shows, including "Doom

00:07:26.320 --> 00:07:30.330
Patrol," "Grey's Anatomy," and a short arc
on "Outlander."

00:07:30.330 --> 00:07:35.000
You'd probably no longer recognize Viggo Mortensen
as the long-haired ranger who launched himself

00:07:35.000 --> 00:07:40.000
into a battle with a group of Ringwraiths
on Weathertop, wielding a sword and a flaming

00:07:40.000 --> 00:07:41.000
torch.

00:07:41.000 --> 00:07:45.090
Now, Mortensen is older and grayer, but still
knows how to command the screen.

00:07:45.090 --> 00:07:48.810
Before "The Lord of the Rings," Mortensen
had already come into his own as an actor,

00:07:48.810 --> 00:07:51.860
spending the '90s acting in a number of notable
films including "G.I.

00:07:51.860 --> 00:07:55.480
Jane," "A Perfect Murder," and "A Walk on
the Moon."

00:07:55.480 --> 00:07:58.870
Nevertheless, Peter Jackson's award-winning
trilogy cemented the Danish-American actor's

00:07:58.870 --> 00:08:00.480
place in the film industry.

00:08:00.480 --> 00:08:02.430
“It’s exhausting being me.”

00:08:02.430 --> 00:08:07.320
Mortensen later acted in David Cronenberg
films "A History of Violence" and "Eastern

00:08:07.320 --> 00:08:11.320
Promises," the latter earning him an Academy
Award nomination for Best Actor.

00:08:11.320 --> 00:08:16.020
Working well together, the director and actor
teamed up again for 2011s "A Dangerous Method,"

00:08:16.020 --> 00:08:20.130
which landed Mortensen a Golden Globe nomination
for Best Supporting Actor.

00:08:20.130 --> 00:08:25.080
Then in 2018, Mortensen starred alongside
Mahershala Ali in "Green Book," which won

00:08:25.080 --> 00:08:27.680
an Academy Award for Best Picture.

00:08:27.680 --> 00:08:32.039
Mortensen continues to be a highly in-demand
actor, with numerous major upcoming projects

00:08:32.039 --> 00:08:36.320
including Ron Howard's "Thirteen Lives" and
another collaboration with David Cronenberg,

00:08:36.320 --> 00:08:38.089
"Crimes of the Future."

00:08:38.089 --> 00:08:42.690
Before playing Gimli, the ax-wielding dwarf
who refuses to be tossed — except when there's

00:08:42.690 --> 00:08:47.860
no other choice — John Rhys-Davies was probably
best known as Sallah, the affable Arab in

00:08:47.860 --> 00:08:50.180
the Indiana Jones films.

00:08:50.180 --> 00:08:54.220
Since "The Lord of the Rings," however, Rhys-Davies
has appeared in dozens of films, video games,

00:08:54.220 --> 00:08:57.550
and TV series, including both live-action
and voiceover roles.

00:08:57.550 --> 00:09:00.139
"Do you know who I am?"

00:09:00.139 --> 00:09:04.019
Prior to "The Lord of the Rings," Rhys-Davies
was already a highly accomplished television

00:09:04.019 --> 00:09:08.470
actor, with roles in series such as "The Untouchables,"
"Sliders," "Star Trek: Voyager," and "The

00:09:08.470 --> 00:09:09.470
Living Daylights."

00:09:09.470 --> 00:09:13.579
He continued to pursue all types of acting
after "The Lord of the Rings," making appearances

00:09:13.579 --> 00:09:18.430
on dozens of shows, including "Psych," "The
Shannara Chronicles," and "Wizards."

00:09:18.430 --> 00:09:22.370
Rhys-Davies has also regularly appeared in
films, although none as big as "The Lord of

00:09:22.370 --> 00:09:23.589
the Rings."

00:09:23.589 --> 00:09:28.279
Most recently, the definitely-not-dwarfish
actor — standing at a slightly-above-average

00:09:28.279 --> 00:09:32.600
6 feet 1 inch — has worked on a number of
smaller films, including "Kingslayer" and

00:09:32.600 --> 00:09:34.329
"Bad Cupid."

00:09:34.329 --> 00:09:38.509
After getting her start in film starring in
music videos for her father's band Aerosmith,

00:09:38.509 --> 00:09:42.899
Liv Tyler made a name for herself in the '90s
in lighthearted films "That Thing You Do!"

00:09:42.899 --> 00:09:44.480
and "Empire Records."

00:09:44.480 --> 00:09:48.839
She also starred in quieter dramas such as
"Stealing Beauty" and "Inventing the Abbotts,"

00:09:48.839 --> 00:09:52.990
before moving on to play Bruce Willis' daughter
in Michael Bay's cataclysmic blockbuster,

00:09:52.990 --> 00:09:53.990
"Armageddon".

00:09:53.990 --> 00:09:58.000
So it came as a surprise to many when she
was cast as Arwen, the immortal daughter of

00:09:58.000 --> 00:10:02.790
the elf lord Elrond and the love interest
of Aragorn in "The Lord of the Rings."

00:10:02.790 --> 00:10:06.420
But Tyler seized the role and made it her
own, fitting right in among the rest of the

00:10:06.420 --> 00:10:10.910
cast and story, despite Arwen being a much
smaller part of the books.

00:10:10.910 --> 00:10:14.199
Following "The Lord of the Rings," Tyler was
one of the first actors to be recruited into

00:10:14.199 --> 00:10:17.800
the Marvel Cinematic Universe with her role
as Betty Ross in "The Incredible Hulk."

00:10:17.800 --> 00:10:22.230
Although, unfortunately for her, that film
tends to be intentionally omitted from the

00:10:22.230 --> 00:10:27.769
MCU canon by most fans, and Tyler hasn't been
seen in the superhero franchise since.

00:10:27.769 --> 00:10:31.269
After starring in a few more films, Tyler
pivoted to television, starring as Meg Abbott

00:10:31.269 --> 00:10:36.389
in HBO's "The Leftovers" and later as Lady
Isabella Fitzwilliam in the Hulu period drama

00:10:36.389 --> 00:10:37.389
"Harlots."

00:10:37.389 --> 00:10:41.230
Most recently, Tyler appeared opposite Brad
Pitt in the sci-fi drama "Ad Astra," and in

00:10:41.230 --> 00:10:46.689
a 10-episode arc on the firefighting series
"9-1-1: Lone Star."

00:10:46.689 --> 00:10:50.600
Hugo Weaving has a knack for playing ageless
characters, starring as the sentient computer

00:10:50.600 --> 00:10:54.660
program Agent Smith in "The Matrix" before
taking on the role of Elrond in "The Lord

00:10:54.660 --> 00:10:55.829
of the Rings."

00:10:55.829 --> 00:10:59.660
However, the two could not have been more
different, with Smith determined to destroy

00:10:59.660 --> 00:11:04.389
Neo and his human resistance, while Elrond
worked to save the world of men from Sauron

00:11:04.389 --> 00:11:06.259
and the power of the One Ring.

00:11:06.259 --> 00:11:09.519
After starring in "The Lord of the Rings,"
Weaving would reprise his role as Smith in

00:11:09.519 --> 00:11:13.920
the "Matrix" sequels, and also as Elrond in
"The Hobbit" films, but his iconic roles wouldn't

00:11:13.920 --> 00:11:14.920
end there.

00:11:14.920 --> 00:11:19.220
He also played V in "V for Vendetta," Red
Skull in "Captain America: The First Avenger,"

00:11:19.220 --> 00:11:21.810
and voiced Megatron in the "Transformers"
films.

00:11:21.810 --> 00:11:25.910
Weaving's other film roles include "Cloud
Atlas," "Hacksaw Ridge," and the Peter Jackson-helmed

00:11:25.910 --> 00:11:27.430
"Mortal Engines."

00:11:27.430 --> 00:11:31.769
Most recently, Weaving appeared in the Australian
streaming series "Love Me," and as Dr. Bergman

00:11:31.769 --> 00:11:34.119
in the sci-fi thriller "Expired."

00:11:34.119 --> 00:11:37.800
For many actors, "The Lord of the Rings" would
be the obvious high point of their career.

00:11:37.800 --> 00:11:41.999
However, the role as the elven queen Galadriel
in "The Lord of the Rings" is a mere side

00:11:41.999 --> 00:11:45.069
note in Cate Blanchette's impressive career.

00:11:45.069 --> 00:11:49.680
The actor owns enough Academy Awards, British
Academy Awards, Golden Globe Awards, and other

00:11:49.680 --> 00:11:51.730
awards to fill a house.

00:11:51.730 --> 00:11:56.100
She is the first performer to win an Oscar
for portraying another Oscar-winning performer,

00:11:56.100 --> 00:11:59.119
thanks to her role as Katharine Hepburn in
"The Aviator."

00:11:59.119 --> 00:12:01.759
“There we both are.”

00:12:01.759 --> 00:12:07.079
With such accolades, it's clear that Blanchett's
roles on film have been extensive and noteworthy.

00:12:07.079 --> 00:12:11.639
Her Oscar-nominated roles also include "Notes
on a Scandal," "I'm Not There," "Elizabeth:

00:12:11.639 --> 00:12:16.370
The Golden Age," "Blue Jasmine," and "Carol,"
but her memorable performances go far beyond

00:12:16.370 --> 00:12:17.370
that.

00:12:17.370 --> 00:12:21.440
In addition to reprising her role as Galadriel
in all three films of "The Hobbit" trilogy,

00:12:21.440 --> 00:12:25.759
Blanchett has wowed audiences in "The Curious
Case of Benjamin Button," "Thor: Ragnarok,"

00:12:25.759 --> 00:12:28.269
"Ocean's Eight," and "Nightmare Alley."

00:12:28.269 --> 00:12:33.100
In 2020, she starred in the FX miniseries
"Mrs. America," and as part of the ensemble

00:12:33.100 --> 00:12:36.300
cast of Netflix's miniseries "Stateless."

00:12:36.300 --> 00:12:40.220
Blanchett currently has numerous other features
and TV miniseries in the works, and if the

00:12:40.220 --> 00:12:45.499
past is any indication, we're sure she has
many more awards in her future as well.

00:12:45.499 --> 00:12:49.629
David Wenham, the actor known in The Lord
of the Rings universe as Faramir, has been

00:12:49.629 --> 00:12:53.759
busy since his days of fighting for Gondor
and the world of men.

00:12:53.759 --> 00:12:59.689
Wenham broke through into the upper-tier of
actors by starring as Diver Dan in ABC's "SeaChange."

00:12:59.689 --> 00:13:03.999
It was a highly-successful television series
that helped land the actor an important role

00:13:03.999 --> 00:13:07.309
in the second and third installment of The
Lord of the Rings trilogy.

00:13:07.309 --> 00:13:11.290
After Middle-earth was saved, Wenham moved
on to a pair of supporting roles alongside

00:13:11.290 --> 00:13:13.679
Hugh Jackman and Gerard Butler.

00:13:13.679 --> 00:13:18.119
He played Friar Carl in "Van Helsing" and
Dilios, the narrator and warrior in stylistic

00:13:18.119 --> 00:13:19.949
Persian-slaying epic "300."

00:13:19.949 --> 00:13:24.949
In 2009, Wenham went back to the stage, playing
the title-character in "Jerry Springer: The

00:13:24.949 --> 00:13:28.559
Opera," which ran for six sold out days at
the Sydney Opera House.

00:13:28.559 --> 00:13:32.899
The following year, he portrayed Melbourne
lawyer Andrew Fraser's long fall from the

00:13:32.899 --> 00:13:36.179
top in the Australian television series "Killing
Time."

00:13:36.179 --> 00:13:39.899
Most recently, Wenham has had a series of
small-screen roles, including Harold Meachum

00:13:39.899 --> 00:13:44.639
in "Iron Fist," Al Parker in "Top of the Lake,"
Jago Zoric in "Romper Stomper," and Price

00:13:44.639 --> 00:13:46.670
Galese in "Les Norton."

00:13:46.670 --> 00:13:50.709
In between, Wenham has still managed to squeeze
in some film roles, lending his voice to the

00:13:50.709 --> 00:13:55.519
"Peter Rabbit" films and appearing in "Pirates
of the Caribbean: Dead Men Tell No Tales."

00:13:55.519 --> 00:14:00.529
He can next be spotted as Hank Snow in the
Baz Luhrmann biopic "Elvis."

00:14:00.529 --> 00:14:05.439
As Denethor, the merciless Steward of Gondor,
John Noble certainly didn't do much to endear

00:14:05.439 --> 00:14:10.300
himself to fans as his character sent his
only living son on a pointless suicide mission.

00:14:10.300 --> 00:14:13.629
He then attempted to finish the job by burning
him alive.

00:14:13.629 --> 00:14:18.170
Denethor died horribly, first catching fire
on the pyre meant for his son, then throwing

00:14:18.170 --> 00:14:22.509
himself from the highest point of Osgiliath
in "The Lord of the Rings: The Return of the

00:14:22.509 --> 00:14:23.509
King."

00:14:23.509 --> 00:14:27.380
Noble's next major role would prove to be
far more ingratiating, as lovable scientist

00:14:27.380 --> 00:14:30.860
Walter Bishop in the hit sci-fi series "Fringe."

00:14:30.860 --> 00:14:36.220
Noble starred on the series for 100 episodes
between 2008 and 2013, arguably cementing

00:14:36.220 --> 00:14:39.050
that character in pop culture even over Denethor.

00:14:39.050 --> 00:14:43.759
Following the end of "Fringe," Noble went
on to appear in a number of television series,

00:14:43.759 --> 00:14:47.259
including "Sleepy Hollow," "Elementary," and
"DC's Legends of Tomorrow."

00:14:47.259 --> 00:14:52.199
Noble also still occasionally appears in films,
with one of his most recent roles being in

00:14:52.199 --> 00:14:53.199
"Silencio."

00:14:53.199 --> 00:14:58.689
Currently, Noble voices The Diviner on the
animated Paramount+ series "Star Trek: Prodigy."

00:14:58.689 --> 00:15:02.019
After galloping into the "The Lord of the
Rings" films as Eomer [AY-oh-mer], one of

00:15:02.019 --> 00:15:06.429
the fearless Riders of Rohan, Karl Urban went
on to have an impressive career following

00:15:06.429 --> 00:15:08.339
the conclusion of the trilogy.

00:15:08.339 --> 00:15:12.610
He starred in action-packed films such as
"The Bourne Supremacy," "The Chronicles of

00:15:12.610 --> 00:15:14.269
Riddick," and "Doom."

00:15:14.269 --> 00:15:18.670
Urban's next big break came in 2009 when he
starred alongside Chris Pine and Zachary Quinto

00:15:18.670 --> 00:15:20.730
as Leonard "Bones" McCoy in J.J.

00:15:20.730 --> 00:15:22.999
Abrams' "Star Trek" reboot.

00:15:22.999 --> 00:15:27.949
Urban's impressive DeForest Kelley impersonation
won him the favor of "Star Trek" fans worldwide.

00:15:27.949 --> 00:15:32.049
He would go on to reprise the role in two
sequels, with another on the way.

00:15:32.049 --> 00:15:34.850
“You’ll love it.”

00:15:34.850 --> 00:15:39.209
Urban also entered the Marvel Cinematic Universe
as Skurge in "Thor: Ragnarok," and appeared

00:15:39.209 --> 00:15:43.029
in a number of films and television series
in between giant blockbusters, including a

00:15:43.029 --> 00:15:46.500
starring role in the short-lived sci-fi series
"Almost Human."

00:15:46.500 --> 00:15:51.959
In 2019, he began playing the role of Billy
Butcher on Amazon's popular superhero series

00:15:51.959 --> 00:15:52.959
"The Boys."

00:15:52.959 --> 00:15:57.509
With multiple major franchises and a super-successful
TV series under his belt, it's safe to say

00:15:57.509 --> 00:16:02.749
that Karl Urban has been doing pretty well
for himself post "The Lord of the Rings."

00:16:02.749 --> 00:16:07.059
Australian actress Miranda Otto already had
an impressive resume prior to "The Lord of

00:16:07.059 --> 00:16:10.989
the Rings," appearing in numerous films and
television series before taking on the role

00:16:10.989 --> 00:16:13.430
of the fierce shieldmaiden Eowyn.

00:16:13.430 --> 00:16:17.589
First introduced in "The Two Towers," Éowyn
was the sister of Éomer and the niece of

00:16:17.589 --> 00:16:21.899
King Theoden of Rohan, who was not content
to hide from battle along with the other women

00:16:21.899 --> 00:16:22.899
and children.

00:16:22.899 --> 00:16:25.939
She instead disguised herself as a man in
order to fight.

00:16:25.939 --> 00:16:30.519
Éowyn was the only character in the films
to successfully take down a Ringwraith, iconically

00:16:30.519 --> 00:16:35.499
lopping the head off a fellbeast before killing
the Witch-king of Angmar during the Battle

00:16:35.499 --> 00:16:37.439
of Pelennor Fields.

00:16:37.439 --> 00:16:41.379
After "The Lord of the Rings," Otto continued
to work regularly in both television and film,

00:16:41.379 --> 00:16:46.299
with recurring roles in shows including "Homeland"
and "24: Legacy," and a regular role on Netflix's

00:16:46.299 --> 00:16:48.329
"Chilling Adventures of Sabrina."

00:16:48.329 --> 00:16:51.970
Next up, Otto can be seen in the upcoming
modern fantasy "The Portable Door."

00:16:51.970 --> 00:16:56.679
She will also appear in "All the Colors of
the Dark," a tense immigration drama in which

00:16:56.679 --> 00:17:00.089
Otto will star alongside Noah Wyle.

00:17:00.089 --> 00:17:03.829
Several years after playing the captain of
the doomed ship in "Titanic," Bernard Hill

00:17:03.829 --> 00:17:09.260
would go on to portray a much more heroic
leader as King Théoden of Rohan in "The Lord

00:17:09.260 --> 00:17:10.540
of the Rings" films.

00:17:10.540 --> 00:17:14.690
Théoden was first introduced as a frail old
man in "The Two Towers," but after breaking

00:17:14.690 --> 00:17:19.230
the dark enchantment that had been cast over
him, Gandalf restored Théoden to health and

00:17:19.230 --> 00:17:20.230
vitality.

00:17:20.230 --> 00:17:24.660
Théoden went on to lead the armies of Rohan
to victory against the Orcs of Isengard despite

00:17:24.660 --> 00:17:28.960
overwhelming odds, but ultimately was killed
by the Witch-king of Angmar during the Battle

00:17:28.960 --> 00:17:30.570
of Pelennor Fields.

00:17:30.570 --> 00:17:34.610
Bernard Hill had a long and impressive acting
career prior to "The Lord of the Rings," and

00:17:34.610 --> 00:17:36.920
continued to work regularly afterward.

00:17:36.920 --> 00:17:41.130
He appeared in dozens of films and television
shows and earned an Emmy nomination for his

00:17:41.130 --> 00:17:45.780
portrayal of David Blunkett in the television
movie "A Very Social Secretary."

00:17:45.780 --> 00:17:50.990
However, in recent years, Hill seems to have
taken some time off, with his last significant

00:17:50.990 --> 00:17:55.590
role being as the narrator on an episode of
the TV docuseries "Horizon" in 2016.

00:17:55.590 --> 00:18:00.230
Still, Hill does appear to have some projects
in the hopper, with several films in various

00:18:00.230 --> 00:18:02.440
stages of production.

00:18:02.440 --> 00:18:07.410
Buried under piles of greasy black hair and
without eyebrows, you may not have recognized

00:18:07.410 --> 00:18:11.460
Brad Dourif from his roles in significant
films such as "One Flew Over the Cuckoo's

00:18:11.460 --> 00:18:13.800
Nest" and 1984s "Dune."

00:18:13.800 --> 00:18:18.250
He appeared as the sniveling Grima Wormtongue
in "The Lord of the Rings."

00:18:18.250 --> 00:18:23.990
A not-so-secret double agent who was loyal
to Saruman while ostensibly serving King Théoden,

00:18:23.990 --> 00:18:27.740
Wormtongue manipulated Rohan's king to do
the White Wizard's bidding.

00:18:27.740 --> 00:18:32.630
That is, until Saruman's enchantment was broken
by Gandalf and Wormtongue was exiled.

00:18:32.630 --> 00:18:36.600
Wormtongue later betrayed Saruman, too, stabbing
him in the back — literally — before being

00:18:36.600 --> 00:18:38.350
shot with arrows and killed.

00:18:38.350 --> 00:18:42.200
Dourif has provided the voice of Chucky the
killer doll throughout the "Child's Play"

00:18:42.200 --> 00:18:44.940
franchise, and continued to do so after "The
Lord of the Rings."

00:18:44.940 --> 00:18:46.490
“I don’t believe it.

00:18:46.490 --> 00:18:48.070
I just don't believe it.”

00:18:48.070 --> 00:18:53.300
He also appeared as Sheriff Lee Brackett in
Rob Zombie's 2007 "Halloween" remake and its

00:18:53.300 --> 00:18:54.300
sequel.

00:18:54.300 --> 00:18:56.330
Dourif didn't just make horror movies though.

00:18:56.330 --> 00:19:00.100
He's appeared in numerous films, as well as
guest-starred on a number of television shows,

00:19:00.100 --> 00:19:02.800
including "Once Upon a Time" and "Agents of
S.H.I.E.L.D."

00:19:02.800 --> 00:19:06.890
Currently, Dourif is still voicing Chucky
in the Syfy original series "Chucky," and

00:19:06.890 --> 00:19:10.440
has roles in several films still in production.

00:19:10.440 --> 00:19:14.760
New Zealand actor Craig Parker played Haldir
in the first two "The Lord of the Rings" films,

00:19:14.760 --> 00:19:19.780
the Lothlorien elf who first greeted the Fellowship
upon their entry into the mystical woods.

00:19:19.780 --> 00:19:23.230
He later showed up to help support the men
of Rohan in their stand against the forces

00:19:23.230 --> 00:19:25.010
of Isengard at Helm's Deep.

00:19:25.010 --> 00:19:28.950
Tragically, despite the immortality of the
elves, Haldir was slain during the Battle

00:19:28.950 --> 00:19:33.440
of Helm's Deep, driving home for both Aragorn
and the audience just how high the cost of

00:19:33.440 --> 00:19:35.070
the fighting was.

00:19:35.070 --> 00:19:38.800
Prior to "The Lord of the Rings," Parker was
mostly known for playing Guy Warner in the

00:19:38.800 --> 00:19:41.460
New Zealand soap opera "Shortland Street.”

00:19:41.460 --> 00:19:45.900
The role is one he reprised again in 2007
for the show's 15th anniversary, and continued

00:19:45.900 --> 00:19:47.820
to play through 2008.

00:19:47.820 --> 00:19:51.750
Parker also appeared in a number of other
television shows in recurring or main roles,

00:19:51.750 --> 00:19:55.580
including "Legend of the Seeker," "Spartacus,"
"Reign," and "Charmed."

00:19:55.580 --> 00:19:59.500
Most recently, Parker appeared in a recurring
role in the Freeform show "Good Trouble,"

00:19:59.500 --> 00:20:02.100
playing pompous artist Yuri Elwin.

00:20:02.100 --> 00:20:06.050
In a role that was mostly performed through
motion capture, Andy Serkis didn't have a

00:20:06.050 --> 00:20:08.410
lot of screen time in "The Lord of the Rings."

00:20:08.410 --> 00:20:12.560
However, he still came out of the experience
as a household name thanks to his brilliant

00:20:12.560 --> 00:20:15.520
portrayal of the tragic and singularly focused
Gollum.

00:20:15.520 --> 00:20:20.660
Thanks to his nuanced and emotive motion capture
and voice performance, Serkis would go on

00:20:20.660 --> 00:20:24.820
to make a name for himself as the go-to motion
capture performer in Hollywood.

00:20:24.820 --> 00:20:28.690
He later brought to life characters such as
the titular ape in "King Kong," Supreme Leader

00:20:28.690 --> 00:20:35.060
Snoke in "Star Wars," and Caesar in the "Planet
of the Apes" films.

00:20:35.060 --> 00:20:41.510
“Ape not kill ape.”

00:20:41.510 --> 00:20:45.520
But Serkis also continued to act on screen
as well, appearing in a number of well-received

00:20:45.520 --> 00:20:50.660
and highly publicized films such as "13 Going
on 30," "The Prestige," and "Black Panther,"

00:20:50.660 --> 00:20:53.140
along with smaller titles that are less well
known.

00:20:53.140 --> 00:20:57.560
Serkis also stepped behind the lens to direct
as a second unit director for "The Hobbit"

00:20:57.560 --> 00:21:00.350
trilogy, which also saw him reprise his role
as Gollum.

00:21:00.350 --> 00:21:04.510
He went on to direct several other feature
films as well, including the recent dark superhero

00:21:04.510 --> 00:21:06.560
flick "Venom: Let There Be Carnage."

00:21:06.560 --> 00:21:11.490
Recently, Serkis continues to dip his toes
into as many lucrative franchises as possible.

00:21:11.490 --> 00:21:16.320
He had his recent role as Alfred in "The Batman,"
and has several projects — including film

00:21:16.320 --> 00:21:20.240
sequels, original films, and a video game
voiceover role — currently in the works.

00:21:20.240 --> 00:21:22.520
